{"id":"f7cac5f2-ea21-494a-9aa1-cd1817d9a0f8","object":"chat.completion","created":1785022523,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n    for key in (\\\"initial_state\\\", \\\"max_steps\\\", \\\"tool_observations\\\", \\\"transitions\\\"):\\n        if key not in case:\\n            return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n    initial_state = case[\\\"initial_state\\\"]\\n    max_steps = case[\\\"max_steps\\\"]\\n    tool_obs = case[\\\"tool_observations\\\"]\\n    transitions = case[\\\"transitions\\\"]\\n    if not isinstance(initial_state, str) or not isinstance(max_steps, int) or not isinstance(tool_obs, dict) or not isinstance(transitions, dict):\\n        return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n    if initial_state not in transitions:\\n        return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n    for state, defn in transitions.items():\\n        if not isinstance(defn, dict) or 'kind' not in defn:\\n            return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n        kind = defn['kind']\\n        if kind == 'action':\\n            if not all(k in defn for k in ('action','next_by_observation','default_next')):\\n                return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n            if not isinstance(defn['action'], str) or not isinstance(defn['next_by_observation'], dict) or not isinstance(defn['default_next'], str):\\n                return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n            if defn['action'] not in tool_obs:\\n                return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n        elif kind == 'final':\\n            if 'value' not in defn:\\n                return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n        else:\\n            return {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"final\\\": None, \\\"trace\\\": []}\\n    current_state = initial_state\\n    step = 1\\n    trace = []\\n    status = None\\n    final = None\\n    while step <= max_steps:\\n        if current_state not in transitions:\\n            status = \\\"invalid_state\\\"\\n            break\\n        state_def = transitions[current_state]\\n        if state_def['kind'] == 'final':\\n            final = state_def['value']\\n            status = \\\"completed\\\"\\n            break\\n        elif state_def['kind'] == 'action':\\n            action = state_def['action']\\n            observation = tool_obs[action]\\n            trace.append({\\n                \\\"step\\\": step,\\n                \\\"action\\\": action,\\n                \\\"observation\\\": observation,\\n                \\\"state\\\": current_state\\n            })\\n            next_state = state_def['next_by_observation'].get(observation, state_def['default_next'])\\n            if next_state not in transitions:\\n                status = \\\"invalid_state\\\"\\n                break\\n            current_state = next_state\\n            step += 1\\n        else:\\n            status = \\\"error\\\"\\n            break\\n    if status is None:\\n        if current_state in transitions and transitions[current_state]['kind'] == 'final':\\n            final = transitions[current_state]['value']\\n            status = \\\"completed\\\"\\n        else:\\n            status = \\\"max_steps_reached\\\"\\n            final = None\\n    return {\\n        \\\"status\\\": status,\\n        \\\"final\\\": final,\\n        \\\"trace\\\": trace\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements a ReAct loop controller. The payload has: initial_state, max_steps, tool_observations (dict mapping tool name to the observation that will be returned when that tool is called), transitions (dict mapping state to a state object that can be either action kind or final kind). For action kind: has \"action\" (tool name), \"next_by_observation\" (dict mapping possible observations to next state), \"default_next\" (used when observation not in next_by_observation). For final kind: has \"value\" which is the final answer.\n\nThe function should simulate the loop: start at initial_state, step <= max_steps. For each step:\n- If current state is final: set status=\"completed\", final=state's value, and stop.\n- If current state is action: perform tool call: look up tool in tool_observations to get the observation. Record step, action, observation, state. Then determine next state: if observation is in next_by_observation, use that; else use default_next. Update state. Continue.\n- If max_steps reached without reaching final state: set status=\"max_steps_reached\", final=null.\n\nWe need to produce trace of each step (including step number, action, observation, state). The state in trace is the current state before action? In the schema, trace items have state, action, observation, step. Typically state is the state at the beginning of the step. Let's check the descriptions: \"Start at the registered initial state.\" So step 1: state is initial_state, action is the tool, observation from tool, then move to next state. So trace should include the state before action.\n\nThus loop: step = 1, current_state = initial_state. While step <= max_steps:\n- If current_state kind is final: set final=value, status=\"completed\", break.\n- Otherwise, it's action: record step, action=state['action'], observation=tool_observations[action], state=current_state. Then compute next_state. If step == max_steps? Actually we still perform the action at max_steps, then after that we check if next_state is final? The description: \"Stop on completion or max steps and return status, final value, and trace.\" So if we reach max_steps after performing an action, we should check if the resulting state is final? Or do we stop before performing the action at max_steps? The bounded ReAct loop: \"Execute a bounded ReAct loop\" - typically you get a limited number of steps. For each step you can take an action. At step max_steps, you take an action and then you might end up in a final state. But if after the action you are not in a final state, then status = \"max_steps_reached\", final=null. We should include the last action's step in trace even if it leads to final? I think yes: trace includes all steps performed. So we can process as: while step <= max_steps:\n   - if final state: break out (before recording step? Actually we want to record action steps only. If we start at final state, we should not record any action, just set final and status=completed.)\n   - else: record trace entry with current state, action, observation. Then determine next state. Then increment step and set current_state=next_state. After loop, if we exited due to final state detection before consuming a step? Actually if we detect final state at beginning of loop, we don't record an action and we set final and status completed. If we run out of steps, we still recorded the last action, and then we check if the new current_state (after action) is final? The loop condition will fail if step > max_steps. After loop, we need to check if the current_state (which is the state after the last action) is final. If yes, status=completed, final=value. Else status=max_steps_reached, final=null. But careful: we might have entered loop with step=1, current_state initial. If initial state is final, we won't record any action. If initial is action, we record step 1, then after that step becomes 2, current_state becomes next. Then loop continues if step<=max_steps. When step exceeds max_steps, loop ends. Then we examine current_state. So that covers.\n\nEdge cases: tool not in tool_observations? Then observation is undefined. The description says \"Select the observation-specific successor or the default; distinguish missing tools and states.\" Probably we should treat missing tool as an invalid action? Or just use default_next? The tool_observations dict gives the observation for each tool. If tool not present, what observation? Possibly it should be treated as an invalid action and we should use default_next? But the instructions: \"Record the exact observation.\" If tool not in tool_observations, maybe we don't have an observation. Could be that tool_observations always covers all tools? But to be safe, we can handle: if tool not in tool_observations, we could set observation to something like \"tool_missing\" or \"\"? But the spec says \"record the exact observation\" from tool_observations. So we assume valid tool. We'll just use tool_observations[state['action']]. Also, the next state might not exist in transitions. If default_next or next state from next_by_observation is not in transitions, we should treat as error? But we can assume all states exist. To be robust: if the next state is not found, we could treat it as final? Or set status=\"invalid_state\". But the instructions say \"distinguish missing tools and states.\" So we should handle missing states by setting status=\"invalid_state\"? But the output schema only allows status values? Not specified. However, the output contract has status as string, no enumeration. So we can set status to \"invalid_state\" or \"error\". But the typical is to follow the spec: stop on completion or max steps. For invalid states, we can treat as an error and set status=\"invalid_state\", final=null. Let's read the description: \"Stop on completion or max steps and return status, final value, and trace.\" It doesn't mention other statuses. But we need to handle invalid states gracefully. The fixtures likely don't have invalid states. We'll assume valid transitions. We'll check if next state exists, if not, treat as final with value=None? Or set status=\"error\". I'll choose to set status=\"invalid_state\" and final=null, and include the trace up to that point. But careful: if next state missing, we cannot continue, so we should stop.\n\nBetter: follow the strict schema: status must be string. We'll allow \"completed\", \"max_steps_reached\", and also \"invalid_state\" as a possible status for invalid tool or missing state. The problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check that initial_state exists, max_steps integer, tool_observations dict, transitions dict, each action state has action and next_by_observation and default_next, each final state has value. Also that tool_observations contains the tool if needed. If any required field missing, we can set status=\"error\" and final=null. But the specification doesn't require us to output error status? It says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should validate and if invalid, we can still return a JSON with status=\"error\" and final=null? The output schema does not restrict status to specific values. So we can use \"error\". However, the fixtures likely are valid, so we can just implement the loop and for invalid cases set status to \"error\". I'll implement validation: check that case is dict with required keys: initial_state, max_steps, tool_observations, transitions. For transitions, each state entry must have 'kind'. If kind is 'action', must have 'action', 'next_by_observation', 'default_next'. If kind is 'final', must have 'value'. Also initial_state must be in transitions. Also for each action state, the action must be in tool_observations. If validation fails, return status=\"error\", final=null, trace=[].\n\nBut the problem statement says: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: tool_trace, observation_accumulation, bounded_retry, terminal_is_final.\" Those are beyond our current implementation? Actually the task is to implement react_tool_loop_controller. The properties: tool_trace (trace of actions), observation_accumulation (accumulate observations in trace? We do record each observation per step), bounded_retry (limit steps), terminal_is_final (final states are terminals). So our implementation satisfies.\n\nWe need to produce Python code with function solve(case). Return dict with keys: status, final, trace. Trace list of dicts with step, action, observation, state.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nAlso note: The case payload has `_case_nonce` which we ignore.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate input: check if case is dict with required keys. If not, return error.\n2. Extract fields: initial_state, max_steps, tool_observations, transitions.\n3. Initialize: current_state = initial_state; step = 1; trace = []; final_value = None; status = None.\n4. Loop while step <= max_steps:\n   - If current_state not in transitions: set status=\"invalid_state\", break.\n   - state_def = transitions[current_state]\n   - if state_def['kind'] == 'final':\n       final_value = state_def['value']\n       status = \"completed\"\n       break\n   - else if state_def['kind'] == 'action':\n       action = state_def['action']\n       if action not in tool_observations: set status=\"invalid_tool\", break\n       observation = tool_observations[action]\n       trace.append({\"step\": step, \"action\": action, \"observation\": observation, \"state\": current_state})\n       # determine next state\n       next_state = state_def['next_by_observation'].get(observation, state_def['default_next'])\n       if next_state not in transitions: set status=\"invalid_state\", break\n       current_state = next_state\n       step += 1\n   - else: unknown kind -> set status=\"error\", break.\n5. After loop, if status is None: \n   - If we exited because step > max_steps: then check if current_state is final? Actually we have already checked in loop before action. But we might have performed an action at step==max_steps and then step becomes max_steps+1. So after loop, we need to check if the new current_state is final? Because we break only if we detect final state before action. But if we performed an action at max_steps, we didn't check if the resulting state is final because we exit loop due to step>max_steps. So we should check after loop: if current_state in transitions and transitions[current_state]['kind'] == 'final': final_value = transitions[current_state]['value']; status = \"completed\". Else: status = \"max_steps_reached\"; final_value = None.\n   But careful: we might have already set status during loop if we encountered final state before action. In that case, we break with status already set. If we encountered error status, we break as well. If we never broke (i.e., loop finished normally because step>max_steps), then status remains None. In that case, we need to check current_state.\n6. If status is still None after loop, set status=\"unknown_error\" (shouldn't happen).\n7. Return dict with status, final (as null if None), trace.\n\nWe also need to ensure that trace includes all steps up to the point of stopping. If we break due to error, we include whatever trace we have.\n\nLet's code.\n\nAlso note: The case payload may have nested dicts. We'll do validation.\n\nWrite function solve(case):\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # require case to be dict\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n    # required top-level keys\n    for key in (\"initial_state\", \"max_steps\", \"tool_observations\", \"transitions\"):\n        if key not in case:\n            return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n    initial_state = case[\"initial_state\"]\n    max_steps = case[\"max_steps\"]\n    tool_obs = case[\"tool_observations\"]\n    transitions = case[\"transitions\"]\n    # validate types\n    if not isinstance(initial_state, str) or not isinstance(max_steps, int) or not isinstance(tool_obs, dict) or not isinstance(transitions, dict):\n        return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n    # validate initial_state in transitions\n    if initial_state not in transitions:\n        return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n    # validate each transition entry\n    for state, defn in transitions.items():\n        if not isinstance(defn, dict) or 'kind' not in defn:\n            return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n        kind = defn['kind']\n        if kind == 'action':\n            if not all(k in defn for k in ('action','next_by_observation','default_next')):\n                return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n            if not isinstance(defn['action'], str) or not isinstance(defn['next_by_observation'], dict) or not isinstance(defn['default_next'], str):\n                return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n            # check action in tool_obs\n            if defn['action'] not in tool_obs:\n                return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n        elif kind == 'final':\n            if 'value' not in defn:\n                return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n        else:\n            return {\"status\": \"error\", \"final\": None, \"trace\": []}\n\n    # simulation\n    current_state = initial_state\n    step = 1\n    trace = []\n    status = None\n    final = None\n\n    while step <= max_steps:\n        if current_state not in transitions:\n            status = \"invalid_state\"\n            break\n        state_def = transitions[current_state]\n        if state_def['kind'] == 'final':\n            final = state_def['value']\n            status = \"completed\"\n            break\n        elif state_def['kind'] == 'action':\n            action = state_def['action']\n            observation = tool_obs[action]  # already validated exists\n            trace.append({\n                \"step\": step,\n                \"action\": action,\n                \"observation\": observation,\n                \"state\": current_state\n            })\n            # compute next state\n            next_state = state_def['next_by_observation'].get(observation, state_def['default_next'])\n            # validate next_state exists\n            if next_state not in transitions:\n                status = \"invalid_state\"\n                break\n            current_state = next_state\n            step += 1\n        else:\n            status = \"error\"\n            break\n\n    if status is None:\n        # loop ended because step > max_steps, check if current state is final\n        if current_state in transitions and transitions[current_state]['kind'] == 'final':\n            final = transitions[current_state]['value']\n            status = \"completed\"\n        else:\n            status = \"max_steps_reached\"\n            final = None\n\n    return {\n        \"status\": status,\n        \"final\": final,\n        \"trace\": trace\n    }\n```\n\nWe need to ensure that if we break due to invalid_state, we set final=None and include trace.\n\nNow produce the required JSON output: {\"implementation\": \"<escaped Python code>\"}. Must be exactly that.\n\nWe'll escape the Python code properly.\n\nLet me write the entire response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1579,"completion_tokens":4283,"total_tokens":5862,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3430},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":43},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}